WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.734
A dashboard shows the euro to dollar rate, and drawing one rate takes 100 milliseconds.

2
00:00:08.734 --> 00:00:13.380
While it draws 1.0809, three new rates arrive within a millisecond.

3
00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:16.694
1.0813.

4
00:00:16.694 --> 00:00:21.890
A StateFlow holds one value, and each new value replaces the old one.

5
00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:26.032
A slow collector doesn't get a queue of what it missed.

6
00:00:26.032 --> 00:00:27.463
It gets the latest.

7
00:00:27.463 --> 00:00:28.893
That is conflation.

8
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.347
Now set 1.0813 again.

9
00:00:31.347 --> 00:00:32.679
Nothing happens.

10
00:00:32.679 --> 00:00:38.004
StateFlow compares with equals, and an equal value wakes nobody.

11
00:00:38.233 --> 00:00:39.135
And it is hot.

12
00:00:39.135 --> 00:00:43.644
The value sits there with no collector at all, and anyone can read it.

13
00:00:43.867 --> 00:00:45.336
That's right for a rate.

14
00:00:45.336 --> 00:00:51.091
It's wrong for an audit log of payment transitions, where a skipped value is a missing record.

15
00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.616
A SharedFlow is for events.

16
00:00:53.616 --> 00:00:55.230
It has no current value.

17
00:00:55.230 --> 00:01:00.609
It hands every event to every subscriber, and its buffer decides what gets lost.

18
00:01:00.833 --> 00:01:07.034
Make one with default settings, and call tryEmit five times while the audit subscriber is busy.

19
00:01:07.034 --> 00:01:12.452
There is no buffer at all, so every tryEmit returns false, and the events are gone.

20
00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:15.272
Now give it extraBufferCapacity of 2.

21
00:01:15.272 --> 00:01:17.032
Which events get through?

22
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.948
Two events wait in the buffer until the slow subscriber takes them.

23
00:01:23.948 --> 00:01:27.600
The third finds the buffer full, and tryEmit returns false.

24
00:01:27.833 --> 00:01:30.332
With 64 slots, all five get through.

25
00:01:30.332 --> 00:01:34.844
And emit, instead of tryEmit, waits for room, so nothing is lost.

26
00:01:35.767 --> 00:01:41.358
replay is the other knob: it keeps the newest values for subscribers who come later.

27
00:01:41.358 --> 00:01:45.618
Emit five with nobody listening, and the first three go nowhere.

28
00:01:45.618 --> 00:01:48.480
A late subscriber to replay 2 starts at e4.

29
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:50.839
This is Kotlin only.

30
00:01:50.839 --> 00:01:53.213
State goes in a MutableStateFlow.

31
00:01:53.213 --> 00:01:57.889
Events go in a MutableSharedFlow, with a buffer sized for bursts.

32
00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.523
So a StateFlow keeps only the latest value.

33
00:02:01.523 --> 00:02:07.759
A SharedFlow hands every event to every subscriber, and keeps what its replay and buffer allow.

34
00:02:07.967 --> 00:02:08.827
Quick check.

35
00:02:08.827 --> 00:02:10.260
A StateFlow holds 0.

36
00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:14.989
While its collector is busy, you set 1, 2 and 3, and then 3 again.

37
00:02:14.989 --> 00:02:16.996
What does the collector see?
